WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.040
One mouse data set sorted sensory neurons into 11 kinds.

2
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.900
A larger one, three years later, found 17.

3
00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:18.620
A 2024 merger of 31 data sets from six species settled on 18.

4
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.580
Which number is right?

5
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.320
None of the three is wrong, and none is final.

6
00:00:25.183 --> 00:00:31.683
The dorsal root ganglion (DRG) holds the cell bodies of sensory neurons.

7
00:00:32.603 --> 00:00:40.443
In 2015, Usoskin and colleagues sequenced the RNA of 622 single neurons from mouse

8
00:00:40.443 --> 00:00:45.663
ganglia. An unsupervised method grouped them without being told the answer.

9
00:00:46.683 --> 00:00:48.043
Eleven classes came out.

10
00:00:48.043 --> 00:00:54.683
Five were neurofilament-rich, three were non-peptidergic, two were peptidergic,

11
00:00:55.103 --> 00:00:58.003
and one was marked by tyrosine hydroxylase.

12
00:00:58.783 --> 00:01:05.063
Think of a map. A country-level map and a county-level map of the same land are

13
00:01:05.063 --> 00:01:07.903
both correct. They differ in resolution.

14
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:15.783
Zeisel and colleagues, in 2018, resolved eight peptidergic, six non-peptidergic,

15
00:01:16.123 --> 00:01:18.183
and three neurofilament types.

16
00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:26.563
That makes 17. More cells, deeper sequencing or a finer clustering setting will split branches.

17
00:01:27.383 --> 00:01:36.023
In 2024, Bhuiyan, Renthal and colleagues merged 31 data sets from six mammalian species

18
00:01:36.023 --> 00:01:37.503
into one reference.

19
00:01:38.403 --> 00:01:41.223
It holds 18 DRG subtypes.

20
00:01:42.303 --> 00:01:48.123
The authors stressed that preparation, sequencing depth and analysis change how many

21
00:01:48.123 --> 00:01:49.363
subtypes are resolved.

22
00:01:50.543 --> 00:01:54.383
One group is defined by injury-induced transcription factors.

23
00:01:54.783 --> 00:01:57.063
They call it the Atf3 group.

24
00:01:58.023 --> 00:02:01.883
That is a warning that an atlas can mix types with states.

25
00:02:02.983 --> 00:02:05.583
Can a state be told from a type?

26
00:02:06.643 --> 00:02:12.103
Renthal and colleagues cut or crushed peripheral nerves in mice, then sequenced

27
00:02:12.103 --> 00:02:14.123
single nuclei from the ganglion.

28
00:02:15.123 --> 00:02:19.003
Injured neurons lost much of the expression that marked their subtype.

29
00:02:19.823 --> 00:02:25.203
Over a time course associated with axons regrowing to their targets, identity re-emerged.

30
00:02:25.203 --> 00:02:30.483
Timing alone does not prove that regrowth restores identity.

31
00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:33.477
So what is a type?

32
00:02:34.217 --> 00:02:37.437
A molecular group that recurs across data sets.

33
00:02:38.217 --> 00:02:45.077
It also needs an independent anchor, such as an ending, a physiology, a target, or

34
00:02:45.077 --> 00:02:46.797
behaviour after manipulation.

35
00:02:47.877 --> 00:02:51.897
Even then, read it with its state and its resolution in mind.

36
00:02:51.897 --> 00:02:56.557
Read each count with its data set, resolution and state.
